package cajeta.lang;

/**
 * The universal root of the cajeta class hierarchy.
 *
 * Every class **implicitly extends `Object`** once the auto-extend cut
 * lands — until then this type is declared on disk for downstream code
 * to reference, but no existing class actually inherits its methods.
 * See [StandardLibrary.md §Object](../../../../../cajeta-docs/StandardLibrary.md)
 * for the full design.
 *
 * ## Default behaviour
 *
 * The methods declared here are **structural-synthesis targets**: when
 * the synthesizer pass lands, it overrides each method in every
 * concrete subclass with a body that walks the class's field layout.
 * Until that pass exists, the `@Native` stubs below provide the
 * fallback semantics:
 *
 * | Method      | Stub semantics                        | Final semantics                          |
 * |-------------|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
 * | `hash()`    | pointer-identity, XXH3-mixed          | structural field-walk via `DefaultHasher`|
 * | `toString()`| `null` placeholder                    | `TypeName(field=value, ...)` debug form  |
 * | `clone()`   | `null` placeholder                    | shallow field-by-field copy              |
 *
 * Both stubs and synthesis use the **per-process random seed** from
 * `cajeta.hash.Hash.processSeed()`, so hash values are stable for the
 * value's lifetime within a process but not across restarts. That kills
 * hash-flooding attacks against `HashMap` keyed on user-controlled
 * input.
 *
 * ## Deferred items
 *
 * - `operator==(Object)` — needs the `@Native` bridge to handle
 *   boolean (`i1`) return-type coercion before its native stub can
 *   ship. Until then, override `==` manually or use `Hash.identity`
 *   for pointer comparison.
 * - **Override-pair enforcement** — when `operator==` lands, declaring
 *   one of (`operator==`, `hash`) without the other becomes a compile
 *   error. Pair is enforced at the synthesizer.
 * - **Cyclic-type detection** — synthesizer rejects classes whose
 *   field graph reaches itself unless a `@transient` annotation breaks
 *   the cycle.
 *
 * @see cajeta.hash.Hash
 * @see cajeta.hash.DefaultHasher
 */
public class Object {

    /**
     * Returns a 64-bit hash code for this object.
     *
     * The default implementation is **pointer-identity** mixed through
     * the SplitMix64 finalizer with the per-process seed — same path
     * as `cajeta.hash.Hash.identity(this)`. The synthesizer pass will
     * later replace this body in each concrete subclass with a
     * field-walk that hashes every field through `DefaultHasher` and
     * combines results via `Hash.combine`.
     *
     * Subclasses that override `hash()` **must** also override
     * `operator==()` once it lands, and vice versa — the
     * "equal values hash equally" contract is enforced as a compile-
     * time pair rule.
     *
     * @returns 64-bit hash code, well-distributed for use as a
     *          `HashMap<K, V>` / `HashSet<T>` bucket key.
     * @complexity O(1) for the default stub; O(fields) for the
     *             synthesized field-walk override.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_hash")
    public int64 hash() { }

    /**
     * Returns a `String` representation of this object suitable for
     * debug output.
     *
     * The default implementation returns `null` until both the
     * structural synthesizer lands and the `cajeta.lang.String`
     * construction surface stabilizes. The synthesized form will
     * follow the `TypeName(field1=value1, field2=value2, ...)` shape —
     * close to Rust's `#[derive(Debug)]`, sufficient for `println(x)`
     * during development.
     *
     * Override manually to provide a user-facing representation —
     * the synthesized form is intentionally debug-shaped, not
     * presentation-quality.
     *
     * @returns debug-shaped string, or `null` until the synthesizer
     *          lands.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_to_string")
    public String toString() { }

    /**
     * Returns a shallow copy of this object.
     *
     * The default implementation returns `null` until the synthesizer
     * pass can walk field layouts and emit:
     *
     * - **value-typed fields** (primitives, structs, enums) — copied
     *   by `memcpy` of the bits
     * - **class-typed fields** — copied by reference (both originals
     *   then point at the same heap instance, matching Java's shallow
     *   `Object.clone()`)
     *
     * Override `clone()` manually to deep-copy class-typed fields when
     * that's the right thing. The base return type is `Object`;
     * subclass overrides narrow it to the declaring class at every
     * site, so call-site code doesn't need a cast.
     *
     * @returns a shallow copy of this object, or `null` until the
     *          synthesizer lands.
     */
    @Native("__cajeta_object_clone")
    public Object clone() { }
}
